          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My mother has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is on four heart-failure medicines, and her ankles are swelling again. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about those drug classes?",
          "answer": "**If your mother has chest pain or discomfort, call emergency services now.** Do not drive herself. Chest discomfort can be a heart attack, and treatment works best in the first minutes. The emergency flag in my assessment came from the words \"chest pain\" in my search, not from anything you described. If she has no chest symptoms, that flag does not apply to her.\n\n**Call emergency services now if she has severe breathing difficulty.** The guidance lists heart failure with fluid in the lungs as a cause of acute severe breathlessness. Keep her sitting upright while you wait. Other signs that need emergency care now, without waiting for an appointment, include breathlessness at rest or breathlessness that wakes her from sleep and does not ease when she sits up, fainting, confusion, a very fast or irregular pulse with symptoms, and pink frothy spit with severe breathlessness.\n\n**Contact her clinician the same day if:**\n- Her weight rises by about 2 to 3 kg (4 to 6 lb) over a few days. The guidance calls this a fluid signal and a reason to act.\n- Her ankle swelling is getting worse, or new breathlessness is developing on usual activity or when lying flat.\n- She wakes at night short of breath.\n- She is passing much less urine than usual. I could not find passages that list this as a heart failure warning sign, so tell her clinician about it rather than assuming it is or is not significant.\n\nDaily weighing (same scale, same time of day) is a useful early warning, because the heart failure guidance says weight gain often comes before swelling or breathlessness.\n\n**The four drug classes.** The heart failure guidance describes four foundational classes for reduced ejection fraction, meaning a pumping strength of 40 percent or below:\n1. **Renin-angiotensin system blockers:** an angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI) where suitable, otherwise an ACE inhibitor, otherwise an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB).\n2. **Beta-blockers** with heart-failure-specific evidence, started when the person is not congested, because starting during congestion can worsen it.\n3. **Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists**, with potassium and kidney function monitored.\n4. **SGLT2 inhibitors**, used for heart failure whether or not diabetes is present.\n\nThe guidance says these are started together or in rapid sequence, and each is raised toward target doses as blood pressure, kidney function and potassium allow, with review about every two weeks during that process.\n\nI could not retrieve guidance on how these medicines affect fluid, so I cannot say whether any of her four could be contributing to the swelling. Please ask her doctor or pharmacist that question directly. Do not change or stop any medicine without advice from her clinician.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The passages I found do not show any disagreement about sequencing or targets for these heart failure classes. The disagreements I found concern blood pressure, cholesterol and diabetes targets, not heart failure. The passages also give no numeric targets for these medicines and do not describe how strong the evidence behind each class is. Her heart team can tell her which targets apply to her.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
